<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 5: AI and Bias  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--
  NOTES (see also notes/ch5-research.md):
  - Case study: AI-writing detectors and students (Stanford study, 2023;
    Daily Nation, September 2025). The Stanford study did not test Kenyan
    writers. The chapter says so.
  - Videos: "3 types of bias in AI | Machine learning" (transcript read; the
    channel is unknown), and the
    three UCLA "AI & Bias" videos (titles only; their descriptions come
    from the Moodle course text).
  - Podcasts: episode 49 (the "delve" backlash) and episode 35 (AI images).
    Not used here, kept for other chapters: 17 (Simpson's paradox, Dutch case),
    104 (misusing data), 268 (AI as a product, with Kate).
  - The Swahili safety question (WSJ, Karen Hao): evidence is mixed.
  - Practise (STACK) section removed on purpose. Questions go in the exercises.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-ai-and-bias" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>AI and Bias</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-ai-and-bias">
        <p>
            Victor Onyambu studies political science at Moi University in
            Nairobi.
            He handed in a four-page essay.
            His lecturer told him it looked like it was written by AI.
            She pointed to the <q>AI font</q> of the introduction, the body
            and the conclusion, and to his grammar, which had suddenly become
            perfect.
            She also said that an AI detector had flagged it.
            Victor sent her his sticky notes, his drafts and photos of the
            library books he'd used.
            She wasn't convinced
            (<xref ref="biblio-nation-2025" text="custom">Ngari, 2025</xref>).
        </p>

        <p>
            The article doesn't say how it ended.
            But it raises a question that this chapter is about.
            If a tool decides that careful, correct writing is <q>too
            good</q>, who does that hurt most?
        </p>

        <p>
            Bias is when an AI system treats some people, or some kinds of
            people, differently from others, in ways that aren't fair.
            It's one of the most talked-about problems in AI, and it's easy to
            miss, because the system looks neutral.
            It's just following the data.
            In this chapter we look at what bias is, the different forms it
            takes, and why the harm so often falls on people who were already
            left out.
            Chapter 6 then looks at where it comes from.
        </p>

        <!-- Draft objectives: revise once the rest of the chapter is written. -->
        <objectives>
            <title>By the end of this chapter you should be able to</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Define bias, and recognise different types of bias in
                        AI systems.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain how biased data or design choices affect what
                        an AI system does.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Look at a case and trace where the bias came from, and
                        what it caused.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Suggest ethical steps to reduce bias in future AI
                        tools.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 1. Case study                                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-ai-and-bias-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: Written by a Human?</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-accused">
            <title>Students accused</title>

            <p>
                Victor isn't the only one.
                In September 2025 the Daily Nation reported on students at
                several Kenyan universities who had been accused of using AI
                (<xref ref="biblio-nation-2025" text="custom">Ngari, 2025</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Ian Thuku, who is 20 and studies graphic design at KCA
                University, was accused of using AI on a 10-mark write-up.
                His lecturer's suspicion was based on his abbreviations and his
                style.
                Ian ran his own paper through a detector called GPTZero, and it
                said that it was 0% AI.
                He says the lecturer then accepted his explanation, but that
                about ten others in his class were accused too.
                One, who scored 100% AI, was told to repeat the assignment.
            </p>

            <p>
                At Kenyatta University, Daniel Chacha says a class of about 200
                was accused over a 15-mark assignment.
            </p>

            <p>
                A lecturer at Rongo University, Dr James Mwita, told the
                paper what he looks for.
                It's foreign examples, leftover template wording, and phrases
                like <q>delve into</q>.
                He says it's harder than checking for plagiarism, and he
                doesn't want a ban.
                When he's suspicious, he tests students in class.
            </p>

            <p>
                So lecturers are using detectors, and sometimes just their
                instinct, to decide who wrote what.
                How reliable is that?
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-how-detectors">
            <title>How a detector decides</title>

            <p>
                Most of these tools work by measuring how predictable a piece of
                writing is.
                The measure is called <term>perplexity</term>.
                It's a score for how surprised a language model is by your
                words.
                Text that's easy to predict gets a low score, and the detector
                reads that as a sign that a machine wrote it
                (<xref ref="biblio-liang-2023" text="custom">Liang et al., 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                That works if people always write in surprising ways and
                machines never do.
                But plenty of people write in plain, predictable ways, especially
                when they're being careful.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-stanford">
            <title>What the Stanford study found</title>

            <p>
                In 2023, a team at Stanford tested seven AI detectors on 91
                essays that had been written for the TOEFL, an English test
                taken by people who don't have English as their first
                language.
                The essays were taken from a Chinese educational forum.
                They were all written by people, and before 2021
                (<xref ref="biblio-liang-2023" text="custom">Liang et al., 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                On average, the detectors said that 61% of them were written
                by AI.
                All seven agreed on about a fifth, 18 of the 91.
                At least one detector flagged 89 of the 91, which is nearly
                98%.
                When the team tried the same detectors on 88 essays by US
                eighth-graders, the results were close to perfect.
            </p>

            <p>
                The team's explanation is perplexity.
                The essays that all seven detectors flagged had lower
                perplexity, which means more predictable word choices.
                They tried two tests to check.
                First, they asked ChatGPT to improve the word choices in
                the TOEFL essays.
                The share flagged as AI dropped from 61% to 12%.
                Second, they asked ChatGPT to simplify the US eighth-graders'
                essays.
                The share flagged rose from 5% to 57%.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    Think about what that second test shows.
                    The detectors weren't spotting who wrote the text.
                    They were spotting how simple it was.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                The authors say the study was a pilot, with small samples.
                Most of the detectors they tested were built on an older model
                (GPT-2), and newer ones might behave differently.
                They still strongly advise against using these detectors to
                judge students
                (<xref ref="biblio-liang-2023" text="custom">Liang et al., 2023</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-not-shown">
            <title>What the study doesn't show</title>

            <p>
                We should be careful here.
                The Stanford team didn't test Kenyan writers.
                They don't report what first languages the essay writers had.
                English is an official language in Kenya, and many Kenyan students
                have used it at school for most of their lives.
                So we can't say that the same thing happens to them, or by
                how much.
            </p>

            <p>
                The Nation's stories show detectors being used as proof, and
                being questioned.
                They don't show that Kenyan writers are flagged more often
                than others.
                That would need a test, and as far as we found, nobody has
                published one.
                Turnitin, one of the biggest tools, says it was trained on
                writing from many languages, so it shouldn't show this bias.
                Its own research hadn't been published when The Markup asked,
                and Turnitin wasn't one of the seven detectors that Stanford
                tested
                (<xref ref="biblio-markup-2023" text="custom">Mathewson, 2023</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-responses">
            <title>What some institutions did</title>

            <p>
                In 2023 the University of Pittsburgh switched off AI detection,
                because of false positives.
                OpenAI shut down its own AI-text detector at the end of July
                that year
                (<xref ref="biblio-markup-2023" text="custom">Mathewson, 2023</xref>).
                In 2025, the University of Cape Town switched off Turnitin's AI
                detection
                (<xref ref="biblio-maverick-2025" text="custom">McKenna and Kramm, 2025</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-trace">
            <title>Tracing the bias</title>

            <p>
                AI systems are shaped by the data they learn from, by how
                they're designed, and by what people do with their answers.
                Here's where each could play a part.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="tbl-ai-and-bias-trace">
                <title>Where the bias could come in</title>
                <tabular>
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>Where</cell>
                        <cell>What could go wrong here</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>The data</cell>
                        <cell>The detector has to learn what human writing looks
                        like.
                        If that writing mostly comes from one kind of writer,
                        other kinds can look strange.
                        We can't see how most detectors were trained, so we
                        can't say whether this happened.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>The design</cell>
                        <cell>Perplexity is built on a guess that predictable
                        writing means a machine.
                        The Stanford tests suggest that this guess is wrong
                        for some people.</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>How it's used</cell>
                        <cell>A score is treated as proof, and the student has
                        to prove they're innocent.
                        We saw the same mistake in Chapter 4.</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                Who is harmed?
                In the Stanford study, it's writers who use plainer, more
                careful English.
                In the Nation's stories, it's students who've already put in
                the work, and who then have to spend time proving it.
                That time is a cost, and so is the worry.
            </p>

            <p>
                Is it the data, the design, or the way the results are used?
                Probably all three.
                That's often the answer.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-lessons">
            <title>What the case shows</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A tool can pick up on something that isn't what it
                        claims to measure.
                        These detectors were measuring how predictable the
                        writing is, and calling it <q>AI</q>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Bias can hide in a number.
                        A tool can be right most of the time, and still be wrong
                        far more often for one group.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The people who get flagged aren't always the ones who
                        did something wrong.
                        And people who really do use AI can avoid being flagged.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Before we say a tool is biased against a group, we
                        have to test it on that group.
                        For Kenyan writers, that test is still missing.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 2. Videos: types of bias                                       -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-ai-and-bias-types">
        <title>Different Types of Bias</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-ai-and-bias-types">
            <p>
                Watch this short video first.
                It's only a couple of minutes long, and it gives names to three
                ways that bias gets into a system
                (<xref ref="biblio-bias-video" text="custom">3 types of bias in AI, n.d.</xref>).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ai-and-bias-video-types">
                <caption>3 types of bias in AI</caption>
                <video xml:id="vid-ai-and-bias-types" youtube="59bMh59JQDo" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-video-points">
            <title>What the video says</title>

            <p>
                The video starts with a game.
                You close your eyes and picture a shoe.
                Each of us pictures a different shoe, and we may not know why.
                If you then teach a computer to recognise a shoe, you might pass
                your own bias on to it.
            </p>

            <p>
                Machine learning means that computers find patterns in
                data, instead of people writing out every step.
                It's easy to think that means there's no human bias in it.
                The video's answer is that being based on data doesn't make
                something neutral.
                We can't completely separate ourselves from our own bias, so it
                ends up in the things we build.
            </p>

            <p>
                It names three types.
            </p>

            <definition xml:id="def-ai-and-bias-interaction">
                <title>Interaction bias</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Bias that comes from the way people use a system.
                        In the video's example, people were asked to draw shoes
                        for a computer.
                        Most drew the same kind, so as more people played, the
                        computer stopped recognising other kinds.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <definition xml:id="def-ai-and-bias-latent">
                <title>Latent bias</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Bias that's hidden in the data, because of how things
                        have been in the past.
                        If you train a computer to recognise a physicist using
                        pictures of past physicists, it will lean towards men.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <definition xml:id="def-ai-and-bias-selection">
                <title>Selection bias</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Bias that comes from which data you choose.
                        If you train a model to recognise faces, you need to
                        check that the photos you select represent everyone,
                        whether they come from the internet or your own photo
                        library.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <p>
                The video ends by saying that there's no magic bullet, but that
                it starts with being aware of it.
                Can you see any of these three in the detector case?
                Exercise 1 asks you to think it through.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-ucla">
            <title>The UCLA series</title>

            <p>
                The next three videos are a short series from the UCLA Institute
                for Technology, Law and Policy.
                Together they explain what algorithms are, how they can fail, and
                what can be done about it
                (<xref ref="biblio-ucla" text="custom">UCLA Institute for Technology, Law and Policy, n.d.</xref>).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ai-and-bias-video-ucla-1">
                <caption>AI and Bias: What are algorithms and how do they work?</caption>
                <video xml:id="vid-ai-and-bias-ucla-1" youtube="8tfKdxo8Rj8" width="100%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The first episode explains that an algorithm is a set of
                step-by-step instructions for a computer to solve a problem.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ai-and-bias-video-ucla-2">
                <caption>AI and Bias: When algorithms don't work</caption>
                <video xml:id="vid-ai-and-bias-ucla-2" youtube="FD-4yC95iZY" width="100%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The second looks at the limits of algorithms, and at how they can
                fail and give biased or inaccurate results.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ai-and-bias-video-ucla-3">
                <caption>AI and Bias: So what's the solution?</caption>
                <video xml:id="vid-ai-and-bias-ucla-3" youtube="xvb_A_qzXo4" width="100%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The third looks at ways to reduce bias.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 3. Podcast 49: words under suspicion                           -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-ai-and-bias-words">
        <title>Words Under Suspicion</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-ai-and-bias-words">
            <p>
                The ideas in this section come from a conversation between me
                and David Stern, from the IDEMS Podcast
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-049" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 49, n.d.</xref>).
                It's about half an hour long.
                It starts with a single word, and it's available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/049-artificial-words-the-unseen-bias-of-ai-in-everyday-language/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ai-and-bias-podcast-049">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 49: Artificial Words, The Unseen Bias of AI in Everyday Language</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-ai-and-bias-podcast-049"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/3795/049-artificial-words-the-unseen-bias-of-ai-in-everyday-language.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-delve">
            <title>The word <q>delve</q></title>

            <p>
                In early April 2024, someone said that they would ignore any
                email that contained words like <q>delve</q>, because it was a
                sign that ChatGPT had written it.
                Plenty of people were upset.
                David says that people in Nigeria answered by posting
                WhatsApp screenshots of themselves using the word in ordinary
                conversations.
                In the episode I read out a comment from one of them, that
                Nigerian English is a dialect in its own right.
            </p>

            <p>
                David has two objections.
                First, even if the sender did use ChatGPT to put their ideas
                into words, he says that's fine.
                Second, using a single word as evidence is ignorant.
                A tool may use <q>delve</q> more often than the people in one
                person's circle do.
                That doesn't mean others outside that circle don't.
            </p>

            <p>
                He also says that the signs of AI writing may only be signs of
                someone who isn't the norm in your context.
                And that trying to catch AI by its wording is a battle that
                people will lose.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-bias-vs-discrimination">
            <title>Bias and discrimination</title>

            <p>
                David makes a distinction that I find useful.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    In David's words, bias is something inside the algorithm,
                    for example a lean towards language that it knows well.
                    Discrimination is the human choice about what to do with that
                    bias.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                That's why it matters who uses the tool, and how.
                A detector that's slightly off becomes discrimination when
                someone treats its result as proof.
                David says that if the tool starts to be used that way, he
                expects it to end up in court, and he's read about cases
                already.
            </p>

            <p>
                He describes the worst of it like this.
                People who use AI skilfully can avoid being flagged.
                People who don't use it at all can get flagged anyway.
                He calls that discrimination in its utmost form.
                He mentions someone who wrote an essay on the spot, and was
                still flagged as ChatGPT.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-own-bias">
            <title>Our own bias</title>

            <p>
                I'll admit that the word <q>crucial</q> sets off my own alarm.
                I listed other words that I now think of as ChatGPT words, like
                <q>robust</q>, <q>foster</q>, <q>empower</q> and <q>harness</q>.
                And then I realised that the same words are in a responsible AI
                course proposal that David and I wrote before ChatGPT existed.
            </p>

            <p>
                David also tells us that he's dyslexic, and that a tool like
                ChatGPT would have helped him a lot when he was growing up.
                He doesn't think it's a problem if someone uses it for a first
                draft, as long as they understand it and own it.
            </p>

            <p>
                At the end of the episode I said that it had shown me my own
                unconscious bias, and that I'd try to stop it turning into
                discrimination.
                David says we can all be guilty of it without realising.
                His hope is that being aware of it leads to more tolerance.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-words-summary">
            <title>Summary</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Using certain words as evidence of AI treats a style of
                        writing as a sign of cheating.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Bias is in the tool, and discrimination is what people
                        do with it.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The harm can fall on people who don't use AI at all.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We all have our own unconscious biases, and noticing
                        them is the first step.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 4. Podcast 35: pictures of farmers                             -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-ai-and-bias-pictures">
        <title>Pictures of Farmers</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-ai-and-bias-pictures">
            <p>
                Bias isn't only in text.
                This section comes from another conversation with David, about
                something that happened to me
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-035" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 35, n.d.</xref>).
                It's about half an hour long, and it's available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/035-generating-ai-images-do-ai-implementations-suppress-diversity/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ai-and-bias-podcast-035">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 35: Generating AI Images, Do AI Implementations Suppress Diversity?</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-ai-and-bias-podcast-035"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/3512/035-generating-ai-images-do-ai-implementations-suppress-diversity.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-icons">
            <title>An icon for fertiliser</title>

            <p>
                I was making icons for a course about farming in Africa.
                I asked an AI image tool for a picture to show fertiliser,
                and it gave me a white hand.
                That was wrong for the course.
                I spent about an hour trying to fix it.
            </p>

            <p>
                Being more specific made it worse.
                When I asked for an African farmer, the pictures had flags in
                the background.
                When I asked for a Black farmer, I got a silhouette.
                In the end I recoloured the hand myself.
            </p>

            <p>
                David says the first picture is the one you'd just take if you
                didn't care about context, and that's why it matters.
                He calls it racial bias built into the leading models.
                He also says that regions like the Sahel, which includes
                Niger, Burkina Faso and Mali, are often left out of the data
                altogether.
                That's his understanding, and it's about where the data
                comes from.
                His example is apps that identify pests and diseases.
                They work where they were trained, but nobody has banks of
                images for the crops and pests of the Sahel.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-average">
            <title>The average that fits nobody</title>

            <p>
                David also makes a point about averages.
                These models tend to give you something close to the average, and
                the average doesn't represent anyone.
                His example is a pilot's seat designed from averaged body
                measurements, which then fits nobody.
                (He told it from memory, and he doesn't know which air force
                it was.)
            </p>

            <p>
                So the pull of the data is towards the middle.
                He says that this drags society back towards the average, just when
                society has been moving towards embracing diversity.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ai-and-bias-what-to-do">
            <title>What can be done?</title>

            <p>
                David's view is that AI is neutral, and that how it's
                implemented decides whether it supports diversity or
                suppresses it.
                He says it's technically possible to build diversity in, but
                the companies aren't currently rewarded for it.
                And he says <q>it's the algorithm</q> isn't a defence.
            </p>

            <p>
                For ordinary users, his advice is practical.
                Notice the bias.
                Write a short guide on how to avoid it, and share it.
                Asking for a particular gender or skin colour is a patch, and
                it's not a fix for society.
                And keep a person in the loop who takes responsibility.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 5. Language                                                    -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-ai-and-bias-language">
        <title>Does It Work as Well in Swahili?</title>

        <p>
            In Chapter 2 we met the Kenyan workers who helped to make
            ChatGPT's safety filter.
            In the Wall Street Journal's reporting, Karen Hao raised a worry
            about that filtering, which is that it was done mainly in English,
            so the tool might be less safe in Swahili
            (<xref ref="biblio-wsj-2023" text="custom">Wall Street Journal, 2023</xref>).
            If it's true, that's a kind of bias, because some people would be
            protected less than others.
        </p>

        <p>
            Is it true?
            It's a claim we can test, and the research gives a mixed answer.
        </p>

        <p>
            In 2023, researchers tested ChatGPT and GPT-4 with unsafe
            requests in ten languages, including Swahili, which they
            counted as low-resource.
            On average, the low-resource languages produced about three times
            as many unsafe replies as the high-resource ones.
            For ChatGPT it was 15% against 4%, and for GPT-4 it was 10%
            against 4%.
            English was below 1% for both
            (<xref ref="biblio-deng-2024" text="custom">Deng et al., 2024</xref>).
        </p>

        <p>
            But more recent work complicates the picture.
            A 2026 preprint tested 61 versions of models from five big
            families.
            It found that 22 of them were more vulnerable in English than in
            low-resource languages, so the pattern depends on the model
            (<xref ref="biblio-zhang-2026" text="custom">Zhang et al., 2026</xref>).
            Another 2026 preprint tested Kiswahili and other African
            languages.
            It found that in long, multi-turn conversations, between about 42%
            and 71% of attempts got harmful output from the models it tested.
            The authors also say that machine translation made some of the
            other languages look safer than they were
            (<xref ref="biblio-marx-2026" text="custom">Marx and Dunaiski, 2026</xref>).
        </p>

        <p>
            Both of those are preprints, which means that other experts
            haven't yet reviewed them.
            So it's fair to say that whether a tool is as safe in Swahili
            as in English depends on the model, the year and the test.
            It's hard to measure, and it's worth testing for yourself.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 6. Illustrative example                                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-ai-and-bias-worked-example">
        <title>Try It: Testing an AI Detector</title>

        <p>
            Let's see for ourselves.
            You'll need a free AI-text detector (GPTZero is one) and an AI
            assistant.
            Use your own writing only.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find three pieces of your own writing that you wrote before
                    2022, such as an old essay, a report or a long email.
                    That's before tools like ChatGPT were widely used, so you
                    know that you wrote them.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Paste each one into the detector, and write down the score.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Ask an AI assistant to improve the word choices in one of
                    them, and run the new version through the detector.
                    Then ask it to simplify the same piece, and run that.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Compare the scores.
                    What does the detector seem to react to?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            Please don't paste anyone else's work, or anything private, into
            these tools.
            And remember that a detector's score is not proof of anything.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 7. Exercises                                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-ai-and-bias">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-ai-and-bias-1">
            <title>Investigate a case</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Choose one case from the course so far.
                    You could pick COMPAS, the birdsong app, Amazon Go, the Dutch
                    childcare scandal, the SHA means test, or the AI detectors.
                    You can also bring one of your own.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Which types of bias from the video (interaction,
                            latent or selection) do you think are present?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Give one way that bias could enter the system.
                            Is it from the data, the design of the algorithm, or
                            how people read the results?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who was harmed?
                            Was the bias in the data, the design, or the way
                            decisions were made?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What could have been done differently to reduce the
                            harm?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-ai-and-bias-2">
            <title>A small bias with a big effect</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Can you think of a case where a small bias in one step had a
                    big impact later?
                    What was the step, and how did the effect grow?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-ai-and-bias-3">
            <title>Name the type</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For each example, say whether it's closest to interaction
                    bias, latent bias or selection bias, and why.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            An app that identifies crop diseases was trained on
                            photos from farms in Europe, and it's used in
                            Kenya.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            A hiring tool is trained on the CVs of the people a
                            company hired over the last twenty years, and the
                            company mostly hired men.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            A chatbot learns from the messages that users send
                            it, and a group of users deliberately sends it
                            insults.
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-ai-and-bias-4">
            <title>Predictable writing</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The Stanford detectors flagged careful, plain English as
                    AI-written, and ChatGPT's help in picking fancier words made
                    the flags drop.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What does that tell you about what the detectors
                            are really measuring?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who would be tempted to use AI just to avoid being
                            flagged?
                            What does that do to the idea of cheating?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would you want to see before a university used
                            a detector to judge a student?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-ai-and-bias-5">
            <title>Whose English is normal?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In the podcast, a commenter said that Nigerian English is a
                    dialect in its own right.
                    Think of the English you read and write, in Kenya or where
                    you live.
                    Which words or phrases might a tool trained mostly on
                    American or British writing treat as odd?
                    What could go wrong if it did?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-ai-and-bias-6">
            <title>Bias or discrimination?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    David says that bias is in the algorithm, and discrimination
                    is the human choice of how to treat that bias.
                    Pick an example from this chapter or the last one.
                    What is the bias, and what is the human choice?
                    Who could have made a different choice?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-ai-and-bias-7">
            <title>A policy for your university</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The University of Cape Town switched off AI detection, and
                    Dr Mwita in Kenya prefers in-class assessments to a ban.
                    Imagine you've been asked to write a short policy for your
                    own university on AI detectors.
                    What would it say?
                    What would a student do if they were accused, and who would
                    decide?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-ai-and-bias-8">
            <title>Test an image tool</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Ask an AI image tool for a picture of a farmer, a doctor, a
                    cleaner and a scientist.
                    Then try again with <q>in Kenya</q> added.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Who did the tool draw each time?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What changed when you added more detail?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Why do you think it behaved this way?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-ai-and-bias-9">
            <title>Is it as safe in Swahili?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The research on whether chatbots are less safe in Swahili
                    is mixed.
                    Why is it so hard to measure?
                    Suppose you wanted to test it.
                    What would you need, who would you ask to help, and what
                    would you do about harmful answers you found?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

